course: dictionary
lesson: 12294
title: Convulsive: sudden, uncontrolled shaking
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Convulsive means sudden and violent, and the body shakes in a way the person cannot control. «Mia let out convulsive sobs when she saw her broken kite.» Sobs are crying, and convulsive tells you the crying shook her whole body.
explain: Look at the clue words around it. «Tom gave convulsive laughter when the clown slipped on a banana skin.» Laughter that makes your whole body shake is convulsive. To check, swap in a phrase like "shaking hard" and see if it still makes sense.
explain: In health, a convulsive seizure is when someone's body shakes suddenly and they cannot control it. You must not hold the person down. Keep them safe, move hard objects away, and call an adult or emergency help straight away.
fact: Convulsive means sudden, violent shaking that a person cannot control.
fact: «Convulsive» is an adjective, so it describes a noun such as sobs, laughter or movements.
fact: «Convulsive sobs» means crying that shakes the whole body.
fact: «More convulsive» means shaking more strongly than something else.
fact: If someone has convulsive movements, do not hold them down; keep them safe and call an adult or emergency help.
exercise: «The girl gave convulsive sobs after her bike was stolen.» What does convulsive mean here? | Very quiet and gentle | *Sudden and violent, shaking her whole body | Happy and smiling | Slow and calm
exercise: Which word describes laughter that shakes the whole body? | Tiny | Polite | Gentle | *Convulsive
exercise: Someone has convulsive movements during a seizure. What should you do? | Leave the room and say nothing | *Keep them safe, move hard objects away and call an adult or emergency help | Hold them down tightly so they stop | Shout at them to stop
test: Tom's «convulsive laughter» filled the classroom. What does convulsive suggest? | Silent thinking | *Laughter so strong that his whole body shook | A loud alarm | Quiet giggles
test: Which sentence uses convulsive correctly? | *The toddler had convulsive sobs and her shoulders heaved | He read a convulsive book quietly | The sky was convulsive and blue | The soup was convulsive and cold
exam: Which is the best meaning of convulsive? | Bright and shining | *Sudden, violent and out of control | Gentle and slow | Very polite and kind
